  "result": "I added `click.KeyValue`, a parameter type that turns `key=value` into a `(key, value)` tuple. Invalid input fails with an error containing \"is not a valid key=value pair\". The full test suite passes (1914 passed, 102 skipped, 1 xfailed), including 8 new tests for the type.\n\nThe changes:\n- **`src/click/types.py`**: the `KeyValue` class. It splits on the first `=` only, so `a=b=c` gives `(\"a\", \"b=c\")`. An empty value is allowed (`a=` gives `(\"a\", \"\")`). An empty key or a missing `=` fails.\n- **`src/click/__init__.py`**: exports `KeyValue` so `click.KeyValue()` works.\n- **`tests/test_types.py`**: conversion cases, invalid-input cases, and an end-to-end `--set` option with `multiple=True`.\n- **Docs and changelog**: an entry in `docs/api.md`, `docs/parameter-types.md`, and a new \"Version 8.6.0 / Unreleased\" section at the top of `CHANGES.md`.\n\nChoices I made that you may want to change:\n- The version is `8.6.0`, because the current version is 8.5.0 and this is a new feature. Adjust if you target a different release.\n- Whitespace around `=` is kept as-is, so `\" a = 1\"` gives `(\" a \", \" 1\")`.\n- I did not commit anything.",
